    {
      "card_ids": ["C04"],
      "claim": "Etretinate, a highly lipophilic retinoid ester, has an absolute oral bioavailability of about 40% versus IV, indicating that lipophilic retinoid modifications still remain above the 20% threshold.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C23"],
      "claim": "The 4-hydroxyphenyl amide-related paracetamol has an absolute oral bioavailability of 63% in healthy volunteers, supporting that the p-hydroxyanilide moiety does not inherently confer low absorption.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The query is a retinoid amide (fenretinide-like) combining a retinoid backbone with a p-hydroxyanilide group. Direct retinoid analogs—tretinoin, isotretinoin, acitretin, and etretinate—all show oral bioavailability values of roughly 40–60%, well above the 20% threshold. Although fenretinide itself is not directly measured in the supplied records and may have formulation-dependent absorption, the balance of transferable experimental evidence supports F ≥ 20%.",
